From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niklas Sheth <hi@niklassheth.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 12:33:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: defer GEM-close unmaps instead of waiting for
 the VM

A live GEM close waited for every fence pending on the VM before
removing the object's PTEs. Mesa only closes idle BOs, but it does so
during steady-state rendering (BO cache eviction of entries unused for
about 2 s, up to 32 per pass; frees the cache declines). Each such close
therefore blocked the freeing thread, often the GL or DXVK submit
thread, until the VM's whole queue drained. Under load that was the GL
CTS close timeout; in games it is a likely periodic hitch.

Submissions already keep every mapped object alive through their
mapping snapshot, so the wait only protected the PTEs. A close with
work pending now:
- marks the object's mappings closing, so cover() rejects them for new
  submissions;
- records a deferred entry with a snapshot of the VM's unsignaled
  fences;
- returns at once, without failing the VM.

A per-file reaper unmaps the entry once its snapshot has completed. All
of those fences are this file's own job fences, so signal_finished
queues the reaper when it signals a job of an armed file. File ioctls
reap opportunistically, and teardown drains as before. A bind that
overlaps only closing mappings (Mesa reusing the address) waits for
those entries' snapshots instead of the whole VM; live overlaps keep the
whole-VM rule.

Out of memory or 4096 entries per VM fall back to the progress-based
wait. The exiting-close path is unchanged. Snapshots that never signal
leave the object mapped and pinned, as a failed close did before, but
no longer fail the VM.

The range logic (cover, overlap kinds, readiness, cap) is in
g16_deferred.rs, with host tests in
selftests/drivers/gpu/asahi/test-deferred-unmap.py.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
